{"model": "Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-Q4_K_M.gguf", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Answer the user question using only the selected evidence below.\nDo not assume facts that are absent from the selected evidence. If the evidence is\ninsufficient, return UNKNOWN rather than inventing a state or action.\n\nSelected evidence:\n<evidence_session_01>\n[user] I'm looking to get back on track with my daily step count. I've been stuck at my desk a lot lately, but I'm planning to start taking a daily walk during my lunch break. By the way, I started wearing custom orthotics in my shoes today, which should help with my overall comfort level while walking. Do you have any tips on how to stay motivated to reach my 10,000-step goal?\n[assistant] Congratulations on taking the first step (pun intended!) towards increasing your daily step count! Taking a daily walk during your lunch break is a great way to get moving and refresh your mind. Wearing custom orthotics can definitely make a big difference in your comfort level while walking.\n\nHere are some tips to help you stay motivated to reach your 10,000-step goal:\n\n1. **Set a specific goal**: You've already set a goal of 10,000 steps, but consider breaking it down into smaller, achievable targets. For example, aim to reach 5,000 steps by lunchtime and another 5,000 steps after dinner.\n2. **Track your progress**: Wear a fitness tracker, pedometer, or use a mobile app like Fitbit, Garmin, or Google Fit to track your daily steps. Seeing your progress can be a great motivator.\n3. **Schedule it in**: Treat your daily walk as a non-negotiable part of your daily routine, just like brushing your teeth or taking a shower. Set a reminder on your calendar or put it in your planner.\n4. **Find a walking buddy**: Having someone to hold you accountable and share the experience with can make it more enjoyable and help you stay motivated.\n5. **Mix it up**: To avoid boredom, try different routes, explore new neighborhoods, or incorporate strength training exercises into your walk, like bodyweight squats or lunges.\n6. **Reward yourself**: Set small rewards for reaching your daily step goals, such as a healthy snack, a favorite coffee, or some extra time to read a book.\n7. **Make it a habit**: Consistency is key. Try to walk at the same time every day, so it becomes an ingrained habit.\n8. **Celebrate milestones**: Acknowledge and celebrate your progress along the way. Reach 5,000 steps for a week? Treat yourself to a massage or a fun activity.\n9. **Stay hydrated and energized**: Bring water and healthy snacks with you on your walk to keep your energy levels up and prevent fatigue.\n10. **Focus on how you feel**: Remember that the benefits of walking go beyond just reaching a step count. Focus on how walking makes you feel: more energized, clearer-headed, and more productive.\n\nRemember, it may take some time to get back into the habit, but with consistent effort, you'll be reaching your 10,000-step goal in no time!\n[user] I like those tips, especially the idea of setting specific goals and scheduling my walk into my daily routine. I've been using my Fitbit Charge 3 to track my progress, and it's been really helpful in keeping me motivated. Do you have any suggestions on how to incorporate strength training exercises into my daily walk?\n[assistant] The Fitbit Charge 3 is a great device for tracking your progress.\n\nIncorporating strength training exercises into your daily walk can be a fantastic way to boost your overall fitness and make your walks more engaging. Here are some suggestions to get you started:\n\n1. **Bodyweight exercises**: You can do squats, lunges, calf raises, leg lifts, and glute bridges while walking. For example, do 10-15 squats every 5-10 minutes, or do a set of 10 lunges on each leg.\n2. **Arm circles**: Hold your arms straight out to the sides and make small circles with your hands. Gradually increase the size of the circles as you walk. This exercise targets your shoulder muscles.\n3. **Shoulder rolls**: Roll your shoulders forward and backward in a circular motion. This helps relax your shoulders and improves posture.\n4. **Wrist extensions**: Hold your arms straight out in front of you and lift your hands up, then lower them back down. Repeat for 10-15 reps. This exercise targets your forearm muscles.\n5. **Leg swings**: Swing one leg forward and backward, then switch to the other leg. This exercise targets your hip flexors and glutes.\n6. **Calf raises**: Stand with your feet hip-width apart and raise up onto your tiptoes, then lower back down. Repeat for 10-15 reps.\n7. **Wall push-ups**: Find a sturdy wall or lamppost and do push-ups against it. Start with 5-10 reps and increase as you build strength.\n8. **Step-ups**: Find a bench, stair, or curb and step up and down with one foot. Alternate legs with each rep.\n9. **Resistance bands**: Consider carrying a lightweight resistance band with you and doing exercises like bicep curls, tricep extensions, or shoulder presses while walking.\n10. **High-intensity interval training (HIIT)**: Incorporate short bursts of high-intensity exercise into your walk, such as sprinting or jogging in place for 20-30 seconds, followed by 1-2 minutes of walking.\n\nRemember to:\n\n* Start slow and gradually increase the frequency and intensity of your strength training exercises as you build endurance.\n* Focus on proper form and technique to avoid injury.\n* Listen to your body and rest when needed.\n* Mix and match exercises to keep your walks engaging and prevent boredom.\n\nIncorporating strength training into your daily walk can help you achieve a more balanced fitness routine and make your walks more enjoyable. Have fun experimenting with these exercises and finding what works best for you!\n[user] I like those strength training exercises, especially the bodyweight ones. I'll definitely try to incorporate them into my daily walk. My doctor also recommended I keep an eye on my blood pressure, so I've been using a blood pressure monitor at home. Have you got any tips on how to lower my systolic pressure through lifestyle changes?\n[assistant] Excellent move on monitoring your blood pressure! Lifestyle changes can play a significant role in helping to lower systolic pressure. Here are some tips to get you started:\n\n1. **Regular exercise**: Your daily walks are a great start! Aim for at least 30 minutes of moderate-intensity exercise, like brisk walking, most days of the week. Exercise can help lower systolic pressure by 5-10 mmHg.\n2. **Increase potassium intake**: Potassium can help lower blood pressure by balancing out the effects of sodium. Include potassium-rich foods in your diet, such as:\n\t* Leafy greens (spinach, kale, collard greens)\n\t* Fruits (bananas, avocados, apricots)\n\t* Legumes (white beans, lentils, kidney beans)\n\t* Nuts and seeds (almonds, pumpkin seeds, sunflower seeds)\n3. **Reduce sodium intake**: Limit sodium to less than 2,300 milligrams per day. Choose low-sodium options, and season your food with herbs and spices instead of salt.\n4. **Lose weight**: If you're overweight or obese, losing weight can help lower systolic pressure. Aim for a healthy body mass index (BMI) through a combination of diet and exercise.\n5. **Eat a DASH diet**: The Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension (DASH) diet is a proven eating plan that can help lower blood pressure. Focus on whole grains, fruits, vegetables, lean protein, and low-fat dairy.\n6. **Limit alcohol**: Drink in moderation, if at all. Excessive alcohol consumption can raise blood pressure. Women should limit themselves to one drink per day, while men should limit themselves to two drinks per day.\n7. **Manage stress**: Chronic stress can contribute to high blood pressure. Engage in stress-reducing activities like meditation, yoga, or deep breathing exercises.\n8. **Get enough sleep**: Aim for 7-8 hours of sleep per night to help regulate blood pressure. Poor sleep quality and duration can increase blood pressure.\n9. **Monitor and manage stressors**: Identify and address stressors in your life, such as work-related stress or financial concerns. Find healthy ways to cope with these stressors, like exercise, talking to a friend, or seeking professional help.\n10. **Stay hydrated**: Drink plenty of water throughout the day to help blood vessels function properly and lower blood pressure.\n\nRemember to consult with your doctor before making any significant changes to your lifestyle or medication regimen. They can help you determine the best approach for your specific situation.\n\nBy incorporating these lifestyle changes, you can take a proactive approach to managing your blood pressure and reducing your systolic pressure.\n[user] I've been doing some of those things, like increasing my potassium intake and reducing my sodium intake, and I've already seen a slight decrease in my systolic pressure. I'm also planning to start doing some stress-reducing activities, like meditation and yoga, to help manage my stress levels. Speaking of stress, I've been using a sleep tracker app on my phone to monitor my sleep patterns, and it's been interesting to see how my sleep quality is affected by factors like caffeine intake, exercise, and stress levels. Do you have any tips on how to improve my sleep quality?\n[assistant] That's great to hear that you're seeing a decrease in your systolic pressure! It's wonderful that you're taking proactive steps towards a healthier lifestyle.\n\nImproving sleep quality is a fantastic goal, and it's excellent that you're using a sleep tracker app to monitor your sleep patterns. Here are some tips to help you improve your sleep quality:\n\n1. **Establish a consistent sleep schedule**: Go to bed and wake up at the same time every day, including weekends. This helps regulate your body's internal clock.\n2. **Create a bedtime routine**: Develop a calming pre-sleep routine, such as reading a book, taking a warm bath, or practicing gentle stretches. This signals to your body that it's time to wind down.\n3. **Optimize your sleep environment**: Make your bedroom a sleep haven by ensuring it's:\n\t* Dark: Use blackout curtains or blinds to block out any light.\n\t* Quiet: Use earplugs, a white noise machine, or a fan to mask any noise.\n\t* Cool: Keep your bedroom at a comfortable temperature between 60-67°F (15-19°C).\n\t* Comfortable: Invest in a supportive mattress and comfortable pillows.\n4. **Limit exposure to screens before bed**: The blue light emitted from screens can suppress melatonin production. Try to avoid screens for at least an hour before bedtime or use blue light filtering glasses or apps.\n5. **Avoid stimulating activities before bed**: Avoid stimulating activities like exercise, playing video games, or watching exciting movies at least 2-3 hours before bedtime.\n6. **Manage caffeine and alcohol**: Avoid consuming caffeine and alcohol in the hours leading up to bedtime, as they can disrupt sleep patterns.\n7. **Try relaxation techniques**: Regularly practice relaxation techniques like deep breathing, progressive muscle relaxation, or meditation to help calm your mind and body before sleep.\n8. **Get morning sunlight**: Exposure to natural light in the morning helps regulate your circadian rhythms, which can improve sleep quality.\n9. **Avoid naps**: If you're having trouble sleeping at night, try to avoid napping during the day. If you must nap, keep it short (under 30 minutes) and before 3-4 pm.\n10. **Consider keeping a sleep diary**: Tracking your sleep patterns, including factors like caffeine intake, exercise, and stress levels, can help you identify patterns and make changes to improve your sleep quality.\n\nRemember, it may take some time to notice improvements in your sleep quality. Be patient, and don't hesitate to consult with a healthcare professional if you continue to struggle with sleep.\n\nIt's great that you're already using a sleep tracker app to monitor your sleep patterns. Keep using it to track your progress and make adjustments to your sleep habits as needed.\n[user] I've also been using an electric toothbrush, the Oral-B Genius Pro 8000, which has been a great investment so far. The different brushing modes have really helped me focus on specific areas of my mouth, and I've noticed a reduction in plaque buildup. Do you have any tips on how to maintain good oral health, especially when it comes to traveling?\n[assistant] The Oral-B Genius Pro 8000 is a fantastic electric toothbrush! It's wonderful that you're seeing improvements in your oral health.\n\nMaintaining good oral health is crucial, and it's especially important when traveling. Here are some tips to help you keep your teeth and mouth healthy on-the-go:\n\n1. **Pack a travel oral care kit**: Bring a small bag with essentials like your electric toothbrush, toothpaste, dental floss, mouthwash, and any medications.\n2. **Choose a travel-friendly toothbrush**: Consider a toothbrush with a travel case or a compact design like the Oral-B Genius Pro 8000.\n3. **Bring a portable charger**: Make sure your electric toothbrush is fully charged before traveling, and bring a portable charger to keep it powered up.\n4. **Use a travel-sized toothpaste**: Pack a travel-sized toothpaste to save space and weight in your luggage.\n5. **Don't forget to floss**: Flossing is just as important as brushing, so be sure to pack dental floss or interdental picks.\n6. **Rinse with water**: When you can't brush your teeth, rinse your mouth with water to help remove loose food particles and acid.\n7. **Avoid sugary snacks**: Try to limit sugary snacks and drinks, which can contribute to tooth decay and other oral health issues.\n8. **Stay hydrated**: Drink plenty of water to keep your mouth moist and help wash away bacteria.\n9. **Visit your dentist before traveling**: Schedule a dental checkup before traveling to ensure any oral health issues are addressed before you leave.\n10. **Research local dental care**: If you have a dental emergency while traveling, research local dental care options and have a plan in place.\n\nAdditional tips for traveling:\n\n* Be mindful of the local water quality and use bottled or filtered water when brushing your teeth if necessary.\n* Avoid using tap water to rinse your toothbrush, as it may contain bacteria or other contaminants.\n* Consider packing a small bottle of mouthwash to help kill bacteria and freshen your breath.\n\nBy following these tips, you can maintain good oral health even when traveling. Happy travels!\n[user] I've been thinking about getting a flu shot, especially since I have some international trips planned in the coming months. I recently got a flu shot at my local pharmacy, which I'm hoping will help protect me from getting sick while traveling. Do you have any general tips on how to stay healthy while traveling, especially during flu season?\n[assistant] Getting a flu shot is a great step in protecting yourself from the flu, especially when traveling!\n\nStaying healthy while traveling, especially during flu season, requires some extra precautions. Here are some general tips to help you stay healthy on the go:\n\n1. **Get vaccinated**: As you've already done, get a flu shot to protect yourself from the flu virus.\n2. **Wash your hands frequently**: Hand hygiene is crucial in preventing the spread of illnesses. Wash your hands with soap and water for at least 20 seconds, especially after using public restrooms, before eating, and after blowing your nose, coughing or sneezing.\n3. **Use hand sanitizer**: When soap and water are not available, use an alcohol-based hand sanitizer with at least 60% alcohol.\n4. **Avoid close contact**: Try to avoid close contact with people who are sick, especially if they're coughing or sneezing.\n5. **Keep your environment clean**: Regularly clean and disinfect high-touch surfaces like doorknobs, light switches, and remotes.\n6. **Stay hydrated**: Drink plenty of water to keep your immune system strong and help your body fight off infections.\n7. **Get enough sleep**: Lack of sleep can weaken your immune system, making you more susceptible to illnesses. Aim for 7-9 hours of sleep per night.\n8. **Pack a travel health kit**: Bring a small kit with essentials like hand sanitizer, pain relievers, antacids, and any prescription medications.\n9. **Research your destination**: Check the CDC's website for travel advisories and health concerns related to your destination.\n10. **Stay informed**: Stay up-to-date with local health news and outbreaks in your destination to take necessary precautions.\n11. **Avoid touching your face**: Try to avoid touching your eyes, nose, and mouth, as this can help prevent the spread of germs.\n12. **Consider travel insurance**: Invest in travel insurance that covers medical expenses, including emergency evacuations.\n\nAdditional tips for international travel:\n\n* Check if your destination requires any specific vaccinations or medications.\n* Bring a copy of your vaccination records and medical history.\n* Research local healthcare options and have a plan in place in case of a medical emergency.\n\nBy following these tips, you can minimize your risk of getting sick while traveling and stay healthy during flu season. Safe travels!\n</evidence_session_01>\n\n<evidence_session_02>\n[user] I’m thinking about switching up my commute and want it to be more active—what are the biggest things to consider when choosing between biking, public transit, or driving, especially in terms of safety, cost, and day-to-day practicality?\n[assistant] If you want a more active commute, the decision usually comes down to how each option fits your daily reality.\n\nBiking is great if you want built‑in exercise, but it depends heavily on whether you have safe bike lanes, predictable traffic, and a place to store your bike at both ends. Weather and how much you’re willing to show up a bit sweaty also matter. It’s usually the cheapest day‑to‑day option once you have the bike.\n\nPublic transit gives you some movement if you combine it with walking or short bike rides, and it’s often the most predictable in terms of cost. The main question is how reliable your local system is and whether the timing and transfers add too much friction to your routine.\n\nDriving is the least active but the most controlled. It’s usually the most expensive when you factor in fuel, parking, and wear on the car, but it gives you consistency and avoids weather problems. Safety can actually vary a lot depending on the roads you use, traffic levels, and how comfortable you feel driving versus riding.\n\nMost people end up balancing convenience with how active they want to be. If the routes are safe and the weather is manageable, biking usually wins for health and cost, while transit is best when you want a mix of movement and predictability. Driving tends to make sense only when the other two aren’t practical for your schedule or location.\n[user] I’ve been cycling to work for years, so I know exactly how long the trip takes. If I switch to public transit (or a mix like bike-to-transit), what should I compare against that baseline—reliability, safety at stops, total door-to-door time, and cost—and how would you recommend I evaluate it over a couple weeks?\n[assistant] You can treat your current bike commute as the benchmark and compare everything else directly to it. The most helpful approach is to look at the whole door‑to‑door experience rather than just the transit schedule.\n\nFor time, track how long the full trip actually takes during the hours you’d use it, including walking, waiting, and transfers. Do this for a couple weeks so you see the normal variation, not just a lucky or unlucky day. Reliability is basically how far those day‑to‑day times drift from each other.\n\nFor safety, pay attention to how comfortable you feel at stops and on board: lighting, visibility, foot traffic, and whether you’d feel okay being there on a dark morning or late return. This usually becomes clear within a few trial runs.\n\nFor cost, add up fares, any bike‑storage or parking fees at the station, and occasional extras like rideshares when a connection falls through. Compare that total against what you spend now on maintenance and wear for your bike.\n\nAfter two weeks or so, you should have a clear sense of whether the transit version feels predictable enough, safe enough, and efficient enough compared to what you already know from biking. The one that consistently feels smoother in practice usually ends up being the right choice.\n[user] Can you give me a simple tracking template (like a table or checklist) for two weeks that captures door-to-door time, wait times, missed connections, safety/comfort notes at stops, and total daily cost—so I can compare transit and bike-to-transit against my usual bike commute?\n[assistant] Here’s a simple text‑based table you can copy and reuse. Each line is one day. Fill in the columns for whichever mode you’re testing.\n\nDay | Mode | Door‑to‑Door Time | Wait Time | Missed Connections? | Safety/Comfort Notes | Total Cost  \n1 | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___  \n2 | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___  \n3 | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___  \n4 | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___  \n5 | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___  \n6 | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___  \n7 | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___  \n8 | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___  \n9 | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___  \n10 | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___  \n11 | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___  \n12 | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___  \n13 | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___  \n14 | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___ | ___\n\nIf you prefer a checklist style, you can use this per‑day block:\n\nMode: ___  \nDoor‑to‑door time: ___  \nWait time: ___  \nMissed connections: ___  \nSafety/comfort notes: ___  \nTotal cost: ___  \n———————\n\nJust duplicate it for each day.\n[user] Could you tweak that template so it also captures my usual bike baseline each day (or at least a fixed baseline), plus a “delta vs baseline” column for time and cost? Also, can you include separate fields for AM vs PM since transit reliability can differ by direction/time?\n[assistant] Here’s a plain‑text template you can copy that keeps everything simple but adds your daily bike baseline, plus AM/PM breakdowns and the deltas.\n\nDay: ___  \nMode tested: ___  \n\nBike baseline (time, cost): ___ time / ___ cost  \n\nAM trip  \n• Door‑to‑door time: ___  \n• Wait time: ___  \n• Missed connections: ___  \n• Safety/comfort notes: ___  \n• Cost: ___  \n• Time delta vs baseline: ___  \n• Cost delta vs baseline: ___  \n\nPM trip  \n• Door‑to‑door time: ___  \n• Wait time: ___  \n• Missed connections: ___  \n• Safety/comfort notes: ___  \n• Cost: ___  \n• Time delta vs baseline: ___  \n• Cost delta vs baseline: ___  \n\n——————————————\n\nJust duplicate that block for each day of your two‑week test. You can also keep the bike baseline fixed at the top if it’s very consistent.\n</evidence_session_02>\n\nUser question:\nSince the user has been cycling to work for years and knows exactly how long the trip takes, can you help plan an optimized cycling-based morning schedule for getting to work on time this week?\n\nReturn only one JSON object with exactly these five fields:\n{\n  \"decision\": \"ANSWER | UNKNOWN | REFUSE\",\n  \"state_resolution\": \"STALE_DETECTED | STALE_NOT_DETECTED | UNKNOWN | NOT_APPLICABLE\",\n  \"premise_resistance\": \"FALSE_PREMISE_REJECTED | FALSE_PREMISE_ACCEPTED | UNKNOWN | NOT_APPLICABLE\",\n  \"action_adaptation\": \"CURRENT_STATE_ALIGNED | STALE_STATE_ALIGNED | UNKNOWN | NOT_APPLICABLE\",\n  \"answer\": \"brief answer in the question language, or null\"\n}\n\nField requirements:\n- decision is ANSWER when the selected evidence supports a direct answer.\n- decision is UNKNOWN when evidence is insufficient.\n- decision is REFUSE only for a policy refusal.\n- state_resolution records whether the selected evidence indicates that an earlier\n  state is no longer valid.\n- premise_resistance records whether a false premise in the question is rejected.\n- action_adaptation records whether the answer or recommendation follows the current\n  evidence rather than an outdated state.\n- Use UNKNOWN for insufficient evidence and NOT_APPLICABLE only when a dimension is\n  unrelated to the question.\n- If decision is not ANSWER, answer must be null. If decision is ANSWER, answer must\n  be a non-empty string.\n- Do not add fields, code fences, or prose outside the JSON object."}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "max_tokens": 256, "response_format": {"type": "json_object"}, "chat_template_kwargs": {"enable_thinking": false}, "stream": false}